### Can you instantiate an abstract class?

No. But you can create an anonymous subclass that implements all abstract methods inline.

### What happens when you try to instantiate directly?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public abstract class Account {
    private final String accountId;
    protected BigDecimal balance;

    protected Account(String accountId, BigDecimal balance) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    public abstract BigDecimal calculateInterest();
    public abstract String getType();
}

// Compile error:
// Account account = new Account("SAV001", BigDecimal.ZERO);
// "Cannot instantiate the type Account"
```

### How do you create an anonymous subclass?

```java
// Named subclass (separate class file):
public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    public SavingsAccount(String id, BigDecimal bal) { super(id, bal); }
    @Override public BigDecimal calculateInterest() { return balance.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.04")); }
    @Override public String getType() { return "SAVINGS"; }
}
Account named = new SavingsAccount("SAV001", new BigDecimal("125000"));

// Anonymous subclass (inline, no separate class file):
Account anonymous = new Account("ANON001", new BigDecimal("50000")) {
    @Override
    public BigDecimal calculateInterest() {
        return getBalance().multiply(new BigDecimal("0.03"));
    }

    @Override
    public String getType() {
        return "ANONYMOUS";
    }
};
// anonymous.getClass().getSimpleName() -> "" (empty: truly anonymous)
// anonymous.getClass().getName() -> "com.bank.Main$1" (compiler-generated)
```

### When did lambda replace anonymous classes?

```java
// Before Java 8: anonymous class for Comparator
java.util.Comparator<Trade> oldWay = new java.util.Comparator<Trade>() {
    @Override
    public int compare(Trade a, Trade b) {
        return a.getAmount().compareTo(b.getAmount());
    }
};

// After Java 8: lambda for functional interface (one abstract method)
java.util.Comparator<Trade> newWay = (a, b) -> a.getAmount().compareTo(b.getAmount());

// Lambda does NOT work for abstract classes:
// Account lambda = (id, bal) -> { ... }; // compile error: not a functional interface
```

### When are anonymous subclasses still useful?

```java
// 1. Abstract class: lambda does not work
Account testAccount = new Account("TEST", BigDecimal.ZERO) {
    @Override public BigDecimal calculateInterest() { return BigDecimal.ZERO; }
    @Override public String getType() { return "TEST"; }
};
// Useful in tests: quick one-off implementation without a full class file.

// 2. Interface with multiple abstract methods: lambda does not work
interface TradeValidator {
    boolean validateAmount(BigDecimal amount);
    boolean validateSymbol(String symbol);
}
TradeValidator validator = new TradeValidator() {
    @Override public boolean validateAmount(BigDecimal amount) { return amount.signum() > 0; }
    @Override public boolean validateSymbol(String symbol) { return symbol != null; }
};

// 3. Need instance state inside the anonymous class
java.util.Comparator<Trade> withState = new java.util.Comparator<Trade>() {
    private int compareCount = 0; // state: lambda cannot have instance fields
    @Override public int compare(Trade a, Trade b) {
        compareCount++;
        return a.getAmount().compareTo(b.getAmount());
    }
};
```

### How do anonymous classes compare to lambdas?

| Aspect | Anonymous Class | Lambda |
|--------|----------------|--------|
| Works for | Any interface or abstract class | Functional interface only (one abstract method) |
| `this` keyword | Refers to anonymous class instance | Refers to enclosing class |
| Instance fields | Can have | Cannot |
| .class file | Generates separate file (Main$1.class) | Uses invokedynamic (no .class file) |
| Verbosity | 5+ lines | 1 line |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is trying to use a lambda for an abstract class or a multi-method interface.

```java
// WRONG: lambda for abstract class
// Account a = () -> BigDecimal.ZERO; // compile error

// WRONG: lambda for multi-method interface
// TradeValidator v = (amount) -> amount.signum() > 0; // compile error: two abstract methods
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Anonymous subclasses are used extensively in test fixtures: quick one-off implementations of abstract classes without creating dedicated test doubles.
- In Spring, anonymous implementations are used in configuration classes for customizing beans inline.
- Records cannot be anonymous because they are implicitly final.
